WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.388
ACID is a common way to describe important
properties of database transactions

00:00:05.275 --> 00:00:08.388
You do not need to memorize a textbook definition
but you should

00:00:08.412 --> 00:00:13.612
understand the general idea behind each letter
Atomicity means the related work

00:00:13.625 --> 00:00:18.375
is treated as one unit If the transaction cannot
complete correctly the

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.488
changes can be undone together instead of leaving
only part of the

00:00:21.512 --> 00:00:26.138
work completed Consistency means the database rules
should still hold when the

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:31.738
transaction finishes Constraints valid relationships
and other rules should not be broken

00:00:31.762 --> 00:00:37.800
by a completed transaction Isolation is about
concurrent work Databases

00:00:37.825 --> 00:00:42.975
often serve many users or applications at the same
time Isolation controls

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.212
how transactions interact so that intermediate
states do not create incorrect results

00:00:47.238 --> 00:00:51.875
for other work Durability means that once a
transaction is committed the

00:00:51.888 --> 00:00:55.500
database is expected to preserve that committed
change even if the system

00:00:55.525 --> 00:00:59.962
later restarts or encounters a failure A simple way
to remember the

00:00:59.988 --> 00:01:05.225
purpose of ACID is reliability Transactions are not
only about writing BEGIN

00:01:05.238 --> 00:01:09.825
and COMMIT They are a mechanism for keeping database
changes controlled and

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:15.488
dependable In this introductory course focus
especially on atomicity and the practical

00:01:15.512 --> 00:01:20.600
difference between COMMIT and ROLLBACK The deeper
details of isolation levels can

00:01:20.625 --> 00:01:21.288
come later
